# Artifacts of running build/npm commands from the repository root instead of
# through the Makefile. Ignoring them is a stopgap, not a fix: it only stops them
# showing up in `git status`, and the next bare build recreates them.
#
# /geth is what a bare `go build ./cmd/geth` leaves behind (a ~137 MB unstripped
# binary); `make geth` writes build/bin/geth, which is ignored further up. The
# leading slash is load-bearing: a bare `geth` pattern also matches the cmd/geth
# directory and would ignore the whole cmd/ tree.
#
# /package-lock.json is what npm leaves behind when run in a directory with no
# package.json: it exits ENOENT but still writes an empty lockfile.
/geth
/package-lock.json

# A finished devnet run, kept verbatim so that the "exit 0" path of gate.sh can be
# re-verified by someone else without them having to reproduce a 42-block run -- and
# without anyone being tempted to fabricate a log to test the log checker with.
#
# Only two files are kept, because those are the only two gate.sh reads: logs/geth.log
# and status-before-stop.txt (137 KiB together). The rest of the run directory is
# deliberately not copied: `chain` is 64 MiB of leveldb, and `keys` + `pw` are a
# keystore and its password file. There is no verification that needs those inside a
# source tree, and one that puts them there is a worse bug than the one it checks for.
#
# This must stay ignored. An untracked directory here makes `git status` dirty, which
# makes vcs.modified=true for every binary built afterwards, which is the exact thing
# gate.sh's G8 is trying to read.
/.tmp-gate-run/
/tmp-keyscan/
__pycache__/
